  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe circulation of what's left of Isaias is centered over southern\nWV and will continue lifting to the northeast. Southerly moving\nshowers this morning will become more northwesterly as moisture\nwraps around the back side of the low. Precipitation chances will\nlinger into early tomorrow for areas that are typically prone to \nupslope northwesterly flow. Only light accumulation amounts \nexpected.\n\nMostly cloudy skies are evident on visible satellite imagery this\nafternoon. The edge of the cloud shield lays near Huntsville, \nalong a line from NNW to SSE. Expect clearing from about SW to NE \nas we head into the night and tomorrow morning. Winds may be up \njust enough to keep fog from forming overnight. Southwesterly wind\ngusts up the valley today will translate and confine to the \nnortheast and higher terrain into tomorrow as surface high \npressure lifts up from the gulf, keeping the tighter pressure \ngradient.\n\nFor the coming week ahead, surface high pressure will take hold \nover the southeast underneath a strengthening upper ridge. 500 mb\nheights could reach 593 dam or so by the middle of the week. What\nthis means for us is dry weather through approximately Thursday \nwith temperatures increasing each day. Chattanooga, for example, \nmay see temperatures real close to 90 Wednesday and Thursday, when\nnormally they average 75 for highs in mid October.\n\nA closed low over the west that may eventually become part of a\ndeepening trough over northern tier of the country, will be our \nnext best chance of rain when a potentially strong cold front \nsweeps through next weekend. This will bring a marked cool down\nwith highs dropping into the 60s next Saturday. Although just\noutside the forecast period, Sunday morning lows may bring the\nseason's first freezing temperatures to northeastern parts of the\nforecast area (NE TN, SW VA).",
